# k31rel. docs/cards/k31-mirror-probe.card's run 2 (the fix-1 build) advises every miss before it is touched (faults 16.7k) and still
# steps in 268 ms with the drives at ~3 requests in flight. A bpftrace histogram of fadvise64 over 30 s of the step
# (/root/k31-mirror-probe4/sample.txt): ~18,800 calls a step, 89 % at 4-32 us (cached ranges), 11 % at 128 us-1 ms: a missing
# 512 KiB piece costs ~300 us in the issuing thread, where allocating 128 folios and submitting one read should take ~20 us. The box
# runs with vm.min_free_kbytes 67584 and vm.watermark_scale_factor 10 (MemFree 3.9 GB of 269.5 GB, Cached 255 GB, kswapd0 at 21 %):
# an allocation past the low watermark reclaims in the allocating thread (direct reclaim). This probe raises
# vm.watermark_scale_factor to 500 for one run (kswapd keeps ~5 % of each zone free, ~12 GB, so the advising threads allocate from
# the free lists), the same request on the fix-1 build with the sampler's try_to_free_pages timing beside it; the setting is
# restored to 10 after. No code changes.
kind: calibration
question: with kswapd keeping ~12 GB free, does a missing piece's fadvise call drop from ~300 us toward ~20 us, and what does the decode rate do?
term: decode tok/s @ D=512, n_predict 128, one cold request, with the sampler's fadvise64 histogram (the 128 us-1 ms share) and try_to_free_pages total beside it
unit: tok/s
predict: 4.0..7.0
condition: tools/ref/k31-nvme-probe.sh with K31_PROBE_SERVE the k31mirror tree's fix-1 bloomery-serve, BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=252G, --place bp
  --ctx-size 2048, the first 512 prose ids, n_predict 128, under one lease; vm.watermark_scale_factor=500 written before the start and
  10 restored after whatever happens; tools/ref/k31-wchan-sample.sh over 30 s of the step; the plan line arena=0, host_experts=76910
reason: [derived] run 2's step is ~8 missing experts a layer x 12 pieces x ~300 us on one thread's contiguous share = ~3-4 ms a layer x 92
  = the 268 ms; if the 300 us is direct reclaim, 12 GB of free pages cut it to the allocation and submit (~20-50 us), the layer's
  issue to ~0.5 ms and the step toward 92 x (0.5 + I/O ~1 + DRAM 0.33) ms + cards 10 = ~180 ms = 5.5 tok/s, still before the fix-2
  build spreads the pieces over the threads. The term derivation cannot fix is how much of the 300 us is reclaim against the
  ext4 map and submit; the sampler's try_to_free_pages total answers it
decide-in: the setting goes into rig-log configs (sysctl for the box) and the serving docs name it for a paged model; fix-2 is probed on top
decide-below: under 4.0 with try_to_free_pages near 0 both before and after: the 300 us is not reclaim (the ext4 map, the bio, the
  page-cache insert); the lever is fewer and larger allocations (large folios, or MAP_POPULATE-style reads by a reader thread),
  and the setting is restored. Under 4.0 with reclaim gone but the rate unmoved: the issue is not the term; re-read the wchan samples
decide-above: above 7.0: the DRAM or card term was overestimated; re-read the timeline before fix-2's prediction
# outcome 2026-11-07 (one request, watermark_scale_factor 500 for the run, MemFree 30 GB after the write and 1.6 GB during the step,
#  restored to 10 after): tg 3.74 / pp 3.25 tok/s, below the band, equal to run 2 at the default. The sampler: try_to_free_pages
#  0 calls in 30 s of the step, and the fadvise64 histogram unchanged (2.17M calls, 11 % at 128 us-1 ms). decide-below's first case:
#  the ~300 us a missing piece is not reclaim; it is the readahead path itself (128 folios allocated, inserted into the mapping's
#  page cache and put on the LRU, the ext4 map, one bio) or contention on the mapping's i_pages lock between the issuing threads.
#  The setting stays 10. Next instrument: a perf profile of the step's CPU inside fadvise64 (spin-lock slowpath against
#  __filemap_add_folio/alloc) before any further lever.
